.\" sage-tunnel(1) manpage — mdoc format
.Dd October 9, 2026
.Dt SAGE-TUNNEL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sage-tunnel
.Nd manage Cloudflare tunnels and hostname routes from the terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar COMMAND
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates and manages Cloudflare tunnels with fixed hostnames. It routes
hostnames to services through Cloudflare and points their DNS records at
the tunnel. Its version is
.Sy 0.1.0 .
.Pp
The connector needs only the token; routes live in Cloudflare. Give
cloudflared the token as
.Ev TUNNEL_TOKEN
in its environment, never as
.Fl -token
on its command line: any process list shows a command line. A service
that runs cloudflared itself keeps the token as
.Ev CLOUDFLARED_TUNNEL_TOKEN
in its env file.
.Pp
.Cm create
creates a tunnel or reuses one with the same name, routes its hostname,
and writes
.Ev CLOUDFLARED_TUNNEL_TOKEN
and
.Ev CLOUDFLARED_HOSTNAME
to the required env file. It keeps other lines in that file and sets its
mode to 600. A key already in the file keeps its line, so a reference
below it, such as
.Li TUNNEL_TOKEN=${CLOUDFLARED_TUNNEL_TOKEN} ,
still resolves in compose. It writes through a symlink, and in place when
the file has hard links, so every name for the file sees the new values.
Recreate the service to load the new environment; a restart keeps its old
environment.
.Pp
.Cm rotate
changes nothing unless the env file holds a
.Ev CLOUDFLARED_TUNNEL_TOKEN
for that tunnel and can be rewritten. So a file that holds no token, or
another tunnel's token, stays as it is. The same goes for
.Ev TUNNEL_TOKEN ,
the key cloudflared reads itself: a token there must be for that tunnel,
and it gets the new token too. As in docker and compose, the last line
for a key counts. A
.Li bw:
reference, which
.Nm sage-secret
fills in from the vault, stops it too:
.Nm
cannot write the vault. Then
.Cm rotate
gives the tunnel a new secret and writes the new token to the file.
It prints the command that replaces a vault copy of the old token
without showing the new one.
The tunnel keeps its id, routes and DNS records. The old token
can no longer open connections, but connectors already running it stay
connected until they restart. So
.Cm rotate
disconnects every connector, as Cloudflare advises for a leaked
token, and lists the addresses they ran from: an address you do not run
means someone else used the old token. The tunnel is down until you
recreate the service.
.Fl -keep-connectors
skips the disconnect for a planned rotation. A failure to list the
connectors is only a warning. If a step fails once the secret may have
changed, the error says what it left undone; run
.Cm rotate
again before the service restarts.
.Pp
Routes use proxied CNAME records. The tool replaces only CNAME records;
it refuses to replace another record type. It adds a catch-all ingress
rule that returns HTTP 404.
.Pp
A tunnel configured locally keeps its routes in a cloudflared config
file, where
.Nm
cannot see them.
.Cm list
notes this in place of its hostnames.
.Cm create ,
.Cm route ,
.Cm unroute
and
.Cm delete
refuse such a tunnel and change nothing: a configuration they wrote
would leave out its hostnames, and a delete would leave their DNS
records behind.
Manage it with cloudflared.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width 24n
.It Cm version
Print the version and exit. This command does not require an API token.
.It Cm list
List tunnels with their status, live connection count, and hostnames,
or a note for a tunnel configured locally.
.It Cm create Ar NAME Ar HOSTNAME Fl -service Ar URL Fl -env-file Ar PATH
Create or reuse a tunnel, route the hostname to the service, and write
the connector token and hostname to the env file.
.It Cm route Ar NAME Ar HOSTNAME Fl -service Ar URL
Add or replace the hostname route on an existing tunnel and point its
DNS at the tunnel.
.It Cm unroute Ar NAME Ar HOSTNAME
Remove the hostname from the tunnel and remove its DNS record if it is a
CNAME pointing at that tunnel.
.It Cm rotate Ar NAME Fl -env-file Ar PATH Op Fl -keep-connectors
Give the tunnel a new token, write it to the env file, which must hold a
token for that tunnel, and disconnect the connectors that still run the
old one. With
.Fl -keep-connectors ,
they serve until they restart.
.It Cm delete Ar NAME Op Fl -yes
Delete the tunnel and remove DNS CNAME records pointing at it for its
hostnames. Without
.Fl -yes ,
the command asks for confirmation. In a non-interactive terminal,
.Fl -yes
is required.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width 24n
.It Ev CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
Cloudflare API token. It needs
.Sy Account > Cloudflare Tunnel: Edit
and
.Sy Zone > DNS: Edit
on each hostname's zone. Pass it with
.Cm sage-secret run
so it is not typed or printed.
.It Ev CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
Account to use. When unset, the script uses the account if the token sees
exactly one; otherwise it reports an error and asks you to set this
variable.
.It Ev SAGE_TUNNEL_API
Cloudflare API base URL. Defaults to
.Sy https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The examples keep the API token in Bitwarden. Export the reference once, and
.Nm sage-secret
resolves it for each command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=bw:cloudflare-tunnel-startr
.Ed
.Ss Create a tunnel and write its connector token
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-secret run -- sage-tunnel create yt-transcribe yt.startr.cloud \\
  --service http://localhost:8000 \\
  --env-file ~/bin/youtube_transcript_server/.env
.Ed
.Ss Route a second hostname
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-secret run -- sage-tunnel route yt-transcribe transcript.startr.cloud \\
  --service http://localhost:8000
.Ed
.Ss List tunnels
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-secret run -- sage-tunnel list
.Ed
.Ss Replace a leaked connector token
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-secret run -- sage-tunnel rotate yt-transcribe \\
  --env-file ~/bin/youtube_transcript_server/.env
.Ed
.Ss Delete a tunnel without prompting
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-secret run -- sage-tunnel delete yt-transcribe --yes
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 on success and 1 on error. When run with
.Cm sage-secret run ,
the command's own exit status is returned.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cloudflared 1 ,
.Xr sage-secret 1 ,
.Xr sage-runtime 1
